## `omarchy/` and `diagnose-crash/`: MIT, © David Heinemeier Hansson

Agent skills obtained from [`basecamp/omarchy`](https://github.com/basecamp/omarchy)
(default branch `quattro`). They are redistributed here **unmodified**; `omarchy/SKILL.md`
in particular is kept byte-identical because the +29.3 pt baseline in
[research/bench/](research/bench/) was measured against that exact content, and changing it
would silently invalidate the comparison.

MIT requires the copyright and permission notice to accompany copies and substantial
portions, so upstream's `LICENSE` is reproduced verbatim inside **each** directory rather
than only summarised here. A reader who copies one skill directory out of this repo takes
the notice with it.

> **Check the file, not the metadata.** GitHub's API reports **no licence at all**
> (`license: null`) for `basecamp/omarchy`; the repository's `LICENSE` file is MIT. This
> project has now been misled twice by that field. Departure Mono's API entry says MIT
> while its bundled licence is OFL 1.1. Verify against the file every time.

## `research/assets/fonts/OmarchyFont.woff2`, MIT, (c) 2026 Mark Cuda

The Omarchy wordmark as a typeface. Upstream:
<https://github.com/markcuda/Omarchy-Font>. Redistributed unmodified, converted from the
upstream TTF to woff2 with `woff2_compress`. MIT requires the notice to accompany copies,
so `OmarchyFont-LICENSE.txt` sits beside it and `build_site.py` publishes both.

## `research/assets/fonts/DepartureMono-Regular.woff2`: SIL OFL 1.1, © 2022–2024 Helena Zhang

Upstream: <https://github.com/rektdeckard/departure-mono> (v1.500). OFL clause 2 requires
each copy to carry the notice and licence, so `build_site.py` publishes
`DepartureMono-LICENSE.txt` beside the font and CI fails the build if either is missing.
See [research/assets/fonts/README.md](research/assets/fonts/README.md).

## The corpus

`research/data/problems.jsonl` is original research: problems and fixes written and audited
by this project, each citing the public sources it was checked against. The **records** are
MIT-licensed like the rest of the repository. The **cited sources** are not; they are
third-party pages linked for verification, and their own terms govern them.

It is research, not a warranty. Every record carries an `audit_status` saying how much
scrutiny it survived, and `ok` means "checked against its sources", not "guaranteed correct
on your machine".
